<p>
  The free versions of the major AI tools are good enough to be genuinely
  useful, which is exactly what makes this decision harder than it looks. If the
  free tier already helps, the case for paying isn't obvious — until you look at
  what you're actually buying, which mostly isn't features.
</p>

<h2>The four differences that matter</h2>

<p><strong>1. Which model you reach.</strong> Free tiers usually route you to a smaller model, or give limited access to the strong one before falling back. On short simple requests you may notice nothing. On a complex document or a multi-step task, the gap is substantial.</p>

<p><strong>2. What happens to your data.</strong> This is the one that should decide it for most businesses. Paid business tiers generally commit contractually to not training on your inputs. Free consumer tiers often reserve that right by default.</p>

<p><strong>3. Whether you can administer it.</strong> A business plan means centrally managed accounts: you control who has access, you can set shared instructions, and you can revoke access when someone leaves. Personal accounts give you none of that.</p>

<p><strong>4. Where the work lives.</strong> On personal accounts, conversations and any saved instructions belong to the individual. When they leave, it goes with them — usually noticed only afterwards.</p>

<h2>An Australian small-business example</h2>

<p>
  A Hobart architecture practice runs on free accounts for a year. It works
  well; three staff become genuinely capable.
</p>

<p>
  Then the person who built the most useful workflows resigns. Everything —
  the refined instructions, the saved project context, the accumulated
  approach — was in a personal account tied to a personal email. None of it
  transfers.
</p>

<p>
  They also can't answer a client's question about whether project documents
  were used to train a model, because they never had terms that said either way.
</p>

<p>
  The licences would have cost roughly $1,600 for the year. Rebuilding the
  workflows cost considerably more than that in time alone.
</p>

<h2>A straight comparison</h2>

<table>
  <tr><th></th><th>Free tier</th><th>Business tier</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Model strength</td><td>Smaller model, or limited access</td><td>Full access to the strong model</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Trains on your data</td><td>Often, by default</td><td>Generally not, contractually</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Account control</td><td>None — personal accounts</td><td>Central; provision and revoke</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Shared instructions</td><td>Per person</td><td>Set once for the team</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Continuity when staff leave</td><td>Work leaves with them</td><td>Stays with the business</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Usage limits</td><td>Low, hit quickly</td><td>High enough for daily work</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>When free is genuinely fine</h2>

<ul>
  <li>Personal use, or learning what these tools do</li>
  <li>Work involving no client or staff information</li>
  <li>Trying a tool before committing the team to it</li>
  <li>Occasional use where limits never bite</li>
</ul>

<h2>The decision, simply</h2>

<p>
  If AI is touching anything that belongs to a client, a staff member or the
  business, the business tier isn't really a premium — it's the version with
  terms you can stand behind and controls you can operate.
</p>

<p>
  And the licence is almost never the real cost. Training people to use it well
  costs more, and matters more. A team with paid licences and no training
  reliably gets less out of it than a team with free tools and good habits.
</p>
